<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.9" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="51847-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation + Plan note"/>
    <title>Assessment and Plan</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item><content styleCode="Bold">Acute viral gastroenteritis with mild dehydration (A08.4)</content>. Probable norovirus, with a workplace cluster, settling vomiting, and no red flags. Mild dehydration only. Stool testing and antibiotics are not indicated. [S]<list><item>Oral rehydration solution in small, frequent sips, about 2 to 3 liters over the day, then a normal diet as appetite allows.</item><item>No antiemetic needed now that vomiting has stopped; if one is needed, avoid stacking drugs that add to serotonergic effects or prolong the QT interval with sertraline.</item><item>Restart sertraline 150 mg tomorrow morning if he is keeping fluids down; two missed days are unlikely to cause discontinuation symptoms.</item><item>Return the same day for blood in the stool, inability to keep fluids down, fainting, or passing very little urine.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Obsessive-compulsive disorder, temporary flare with illness (F42.2)</content>. A real infection has given his contamination fears new force. Clear, time-limited hygiene advice helps; open-ended cleaning feeds the compulsions. Safety reviewed: no suicidal thoughts, intent, or plan. [S]<list><item>While ill and for two days after: soap and water after the toilet and before food, since alcohol gel works poorly against norovirus; one cleaning of the bathroom with a bleach solution; then return to his exposure plan.</item><item>Message to his therapist, with his agreement, so the next session can address the flare; a setback during illness is expected and is not a loss of progress.</item><item>Collaborative safety plan reviewed and unchanged; 988 Suicide &amp; Crisis Lifeline and the clinic number confirmed.</item></list></item></list><paragraph>Guideline basis: Practice Guideline for the Treatment of Patients With Obsessive-Compulsive Disorder (2007); VA/DoD Clinical Practice Guideline for Assessment and Management of Patients at Risk for Suicide (2024); 2017 Infectious Diseases Society of America Clinical Practice Guidelines for the Diagnosis and Management of Infectious Diarrhea (2017)</paragraph><paragraph>Follow-up: Telephone check tomorrow by the clinic nurse; return if not improving in three days or at once for the warning signs above. Preventive visit in October. Next scheduled visit 1902-10-13: periodic adult preventive visit. [S]</paragraph></text>

  </section>
